1. Convert all date and time strings into a consistent format (YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SSZ or YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSTZ). This will make it easier to compare departure times and ensure accuracy.

2. Compare the scheduled departure time with the actual departure time for each flight. If the actual departure time is earlier than the scheduled departure time, mark the flight as "Early Departure".

3. Create a new column named "Status" to store the results of the comparison. The values in this column will be either "Scheduled" or "Early Departure".

Here's an example Python code snippet that demonstrates how you can achieve this:

```python
import pandas as pd
from datetime import datetime, timedelta

# Assuming the data is in a DataFrame called df and the column with flight details is named 'flight'
df['flight'] = df['flight'].apply(lambda x: x.split(' | ') if isinstance(x, str) else x)
df['flight'] = df['flight'].apply(lambda x: pd.Series(x))
df['Date_Time'] = df['flight'].apply(lambda x: datetime.strptime(x[1], '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S%z') if len(x) > 1 else None)
df['Status'] = df['Date_Time'].apply(lambda x: 'Scheduled' if x is None else ('Early Departure' if x < datetime.strptime(df['flight'][0], '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S%z') else 'Scheduled'))
```

This code first splits the data into separate columns for flight details and timestamps, then converts the timestamps to a consistent format. It then checks if the actual departure time is earlier than the scheduled departure time and assigns the appropriate status to each flight. The resulting DataFrame will have an additional column named "Status" that indicates whether each flight was an early departure or not.
